{"files":[{"content":"#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <utility>\n\nusing namespace std;\n\ntypedef vector<vector<unsigned char>> adjacency_matrix;\n\nadjacency_matrix readGraph();\n\nbool testIsomorphism(adjacency_matrix &g1, adjacency_matrix &g2);\nbool verifyPermutation(adjacency_matrix &g1, adjacency_matrix &g2, vector<int> &equivalence);\ninline void printPermutation(vector<int> &equivalence);\n\nint main(){\n    adjacency_matrix g1 = readGraph();\n    adjacency_matrix g2 = readGraph();\n\n    if(!testIsomorphism(g1, g2))\n        cout << \"The graphs are not isomorphic\" << endl;\n\n    return 0;\n}\n\nbool testIsomorphism(adjacency_matrix &g1, adjacency_matrix &g2){\n    vector<int> equivalence(g1.size(), 0);\n    vector<int> exchange_counter(g1.size(), 0);\n    int exchange_index = 1;\n    bool isomorphism_found = false;\n\n    if(g1.size() != g2.size()) return false;\n\n    for(int i = 0; i < equivalence.size(); ++i)\n        equivalence[i] = i;\n\n    if(verifyPermutation(g1, g2, equivalence)){\n        printPermutation(equivalence);\n        return true;\n    }\n    \n    /*\n     * Permutations are based on Heap's algorithm, which generates all possible \n     * permutations for a given set of elements. \n     */\n    while(exchange_index < g1.size()){\n        if(exchange_counter[exchange_index] < exchange_index){\n            if(exchange_index % 2 == 0)\n                swap(equivalence[0], equivalence[exchange_index]);\n            else\n                swap(equivalence[exchange_counter[exchange_index]], equivalence[exchange_index]);\n            if(verifyPermutation(g1, g2, equivalence)){\n                printPermutation(equivalence);\n                isomorphism_found = true;\n            }\n\n            exchange_counter[exchange_index]++;\n            exchange_index = 1;\n        }else{\n            exchange_counter[exchange_index] = 0;\n            exchange_index++;\n        }\n    }\n    return isomorphism_found; \n}\n\nadjacency_matrix readGraph(){\n    int vertices, edges, source, destination;\n\n    cin >> vertices;\n    adjacency_matrix read(vertices, vector<unsigned char>(vertices, 0));\n    \n    cin >> edges;    \n    for (int i = 0; i < edges; i++) {\n       cin >> source >> destination;\n       read[destination][source] = read[source][destination] = 1; \n    }\n\n    return read;\n}\n\nbool verifyPermutation(adjacency_matrix &g1, adjacency_matrix &g2, vector<int> &equivalence){\n    for (int i = 0; i < equivalence.size(); ++i) {\n        int row_matches = 1;\n        const int mapped_i = equivalence[i];\n        #pragma omp simd reduction(&:row_matches)\n        for (int j = 0; j <= i; ++j) {\n            row_matches &= (g1[i][j] == g2[mapped_i][equivalence[j]]);\n        }\n        if (!row_matches) return false;\n    }\n   return true;\n}\n\ninline void printPermutation(vector<int> &equivalence){\n    for (auto i : equivalence)\n        cout << i << \" \";\n    cout << endl;\n}\n","path":"src/brute_isomorphism.cpp"}],"hypothesis":"Using byte-addressable adjacency rows and a legal SIMD reduction over independent comparisons within each verifier row may reduce bit-proxy overhead and accelerate permutation checks without changing permutation traversal or output.","notes":"The SIMD loop only combines independent equality results with bitwise AND; it still checks the same lower-triangular entries and returns false at the same row boundary. No commands were run.","research":{"queries":[],"urls":[]},"sources_kept":[],"transformations":["Changed the adjacency matrix elements from vector<bool> proxies to zero-initialized unsigned bytes.","Applied an OpenMP SIMD bitwise-AND reduction to the independent inner-loop comparisons, retaining the original row-by-row early return and Heap permutation order."]}